WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.520
The Yellowstone prequel 1883 was full of&nbsp;
painful surprises and slow reveals. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.680
did you know the finale had more connections&nbsp;
to the original series than meets the eye?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.120
Keep watching for the truth&nbsp;
behind 1883's final moments.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.040
With Elsa seriously injured from the wound&nbsp;
inflicted by the Comanche in "Racing Clouds,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.480
James and Margaret take her to&nbsp;
the nearby fort to see a surgeon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.160
They suspect the wound is infected,&nbsp;
due to the fact it wasn't healing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.680
With Josef and his wife also sick and injured,&nbsp;
Shea agrees to the detour. At the start of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.400
"This Is Not Your Heaven," they arrive at a&nbsp;
small fort guarded by two teenage soldiers.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.640
Their call for a surgeon is answered by Major&nbsp;
Hemphill, who has no staff and no nurses. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.120
examining Elsa, the Major reveals that the&nbsp;
arrow that struck her hit her liver, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:52.480
that she's already gone septic. He tells Shea and&nbsp;
Thomas that there is nothing he can do for Elsa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.040
and that what they thought was&nbsp;
their salvation is little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.880
than a meeting place for ambitious&nbsp;
railroad engineers mapping the region.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.320
He explains that Fort Laramie is&nbsp;
their best option, and not by much.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.640
"She's done traveling. Laramie's as&nbsp;
far as she goes, if you're lucky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.920
Best you could hope for is that she&nbsp;
wakes and you get to say goodbye."

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.080
At this point, it seems the hope&nbsp;
that fans had pinned on Elsa's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.480
survival in the previous episode were&nbsp;
crushed by the Major's sobering words.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.720
When they realize that Elsa's&nbsp;
arrow wound won't heal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.160
James and Margaret clash over what&nbsp;
they should tell their daughter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.800
Drifting in and out of consciousness, Elsa&nbsp;
remains steadfast that she'll recover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.440
and her mother doesn't want to take that hope&nbsp;
away. James, meanwhile, believes in his daughter's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:40.000
strength and spirit, and wants to be honest&nbsp;
with her about the seriousness of her wound.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.360
As it becomes more and more clear&nbsp;
that their daughter won't survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:49.200
tension between James and Margaret arises. It's&nbsp;
unspoken, but James is clearly frustrated that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.840
can't tell Elsa the truth, and that she may&nbsp;
die without ever being able to say goodbye.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.680
Margaret, meanwhile, is still holding on to the&nbsp;
last vestiges of hope that Elsa might recover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.720
She even reassures Elsa that her fever has&nbsp;
broken, and she's looking better. While this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.800
decision upsets James, he still agrees&nbsp;
to lie to Elsa when she confronts them.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:15.200
When 1883 first started, many fans of Yellowstone&nbsp;
must have scratched their heads when they learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:20.480
James Dutton wasn't traveling to Montana, but&nbsp;
instead joined a wagon train headed to Oregon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.440
Audiences knew the train would be diverted at&nbsp;
some point, and began theorizing how and why it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.760
would eventually happen. We finally&nbsp;
discover the reason in the finale.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.960
With the Fort offering little help for Elsa, and&nbsp;
the next destination too far to reach in time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.840
James and Shea decide they must make drastic&nbsp;
changes to their travel plans if they're to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.800
give Elsa a proper resting place. The journey&nbsp;
that was destined to end in Oregon will now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:49.040
divert to the Bozeman Valley in Montana, where&nbsp;
he tells the group they will spend the winter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.840
before departing for Oregon in the spring.&nbsp;
With their numbers dwindling, Elsa dying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:59.680
and Josef and his wife unable to travel, it&nbsp;
seems the best and only option for survival.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.000
Unfortunately, the rest of the group is&nbsp;
adamant that they continue on to Oregon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.440
with or without Shea. The grizzled war veteran&nbsp;
promises he'll still get them to Oregon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.160
but they insist on continuing. Despite&nbsp;
Shea's warnings, their minds are made up.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:21.200
"We are staying. The trail is easy enough to&nbsp;
follow now. We'll make it to Oregon without you."

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.000
"Suit yourself."

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.400
Yellowstone fans now know why&nbsp;
the Duttons settled in Montana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.840
and it was due to a tragedy few&nbsp;
saw coming when the series began.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.520
As the rest of the wagon train departs for&nbsp;
Oregon on their own, Shea stays behind with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:39.840
James Dutton and his family, preparing to&nbsp;
begin their journey to the Bozeman valley.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.720
Shea releases the cattle, and lets cowboys&nbsp;
Colton and Wade depart the wagon and train.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:50.320
After saying goodbye to their companions, they&nbsp;
ride off to an unknown future. Thomas, Noemi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.280
and their children decide to stick with Shea.&nbsp;
Josef and his wife also want to go to Montana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.480
but Shea refuses them, saying they are too sick&nbsp;
to keep up. Noemi demands they be allowed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.200
join them, declaring to Shea that they are&nbsp;
now free people whether they live or die.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.400
She even offers up her own&nbsp;
services as driver of their wagon.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:14.800
As days drag on, and Josef's snake bite continues&nbsp;
to worsen, they're forced to face the stark truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:19.840
that he might not make it to Montana. Thomas&nbsp;
then offers Josef a choice: They can amputate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:26.160
the snake-bitten leg, or he can die with it. Josef&nbsp;
chooses the only real option, and Shea and Thomas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:31.760
prepare to become battlefield surgeons. Getting&nbsp;
Josef drunk enough to pass out, they remove&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.920
Josef's leg in a brutal surgery by firelight,&nbsp;
to which the rapidly dying Elsa responds,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:39.760
"Sure glad I didn't get shot in the leg."

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.000
Before the surgery on Josef, Shea and James&nbsp;
sit around a fire and talk while Margaret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.800
sings Elsa a lullaby. James explains&nbsp;
that she only sings for their children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:55.360
and that she paradoxically promised to sing&nbsp;
at his funeral, despite making him promise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:00.560
he'd die after her. However, it's Shea who&nbsp;
tells the most revealing story of the night.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.360
He finally opens up about his past, and how&nbsp;
his wife helped him survive the Civil War.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.840
According to Shea, his wife back home had sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.960
him a letter explaining that she&nbsp;
was leaving him for another man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.160
prompting him to leave his unit and return home&nbsp;
with the intent to kill his wife's new lover.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:20.800
When he got there, it turned out to be a ruse&nbsp;
designed to get him to leave the war, ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:25.200
saving him from death on the battlefield&nbsp;
at Gettysburg, and reuniting the couple.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.360
It's a touching story, one that gives new&nbsp;
meaning to Shea's longing for the family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:34.880
we saw him leave behind in the premiere episode.&nbsp;
The story illustrates the sadness in Shea's past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:38.160
and the passion that drives him&nbsp;
throughout the series. It also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.800
helps explain the respect he has for James and&nbsp;
his relationship with his wife and daughter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.280
The love he sees in their eyes proves that he's&nbsp;
more than just a battle hardened war veteran.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:53.200
The settlers' decision to proceed to Oregon&nbsp;
without Shea was met with derision from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:58.240
cowboy, who warned them they would die if they&nbsp;
attempted to go it alone. He insisted that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:03.600
best hope for survival was to spend the winter in&nbsp;
the valley and set off for Oregon in the spring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:08.400
but the remaining group had had enough of&nbsp;
Brennan's delays. Even with his protection, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:14.000
had gotten many of them killed crossing a river,&nbsp;
with more dying due to accidents and attacks.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.240
The settlers also felt that at this point&nbsp;
in the journey, the trail was well marked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.000
enough that they didn't need Shea as a&nbsp;
guide — a decision which would quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:28.160
doom them. Not long after splitting up, a group&nbsp;
of bandits attack the wagon train, looting it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:32.800
and killing everyone they found. It was&nbsp;
another grim reminder of the lawlessness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:37.760
and brutality of life on the plains, and&nbsp;
the dangers that lurk around every corner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:41.360
They came to American shores with&nbsp;
dreams of freedom and prosperity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.200
but in the end they wound up meeting their&nbsp;
fate at the end of an outlaw's pistol.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:50.960
Though all hope seems lost for Elsa as James,&nbsp;
Shea, and the remaining members of the group take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:56.080
her north towards Montana to die, they have an&nbsp;
unexpected run-in with a band of friendly Native&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:02.080
Americans. The leader of the tribe, an elder named&nbsp;
Spotted Eagle, sees that Elsa is in rough shape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.600
and says he has a doctor in his own&nbsp;
group that may be able to help her.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:07.840
"What do your doctors say?"

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:09.760
"We have no doctors."

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:12.320
"I'll get you a doctor."

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.720
After days spent injured on the trail, Elsa&nbsp;
finally gets proper attention for her wound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:22.960
from two medicine women who take her to a&nbsp;
nearby stream and flush out the infection.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:26.960
Audiences may have believed this was the&nbsp;
moment when things would change for Elsa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:32.320
and she might finally be saved. It would have made&nbsp;
thematic sense if she were healed by the tribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.040
since she had bonded to a Native American&nbsp;
man earlier in the season. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:42.080
the tribe's prognosis wasn't any better than the&nbsp;
fort's. The leader of the tribe explains that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:46.960
the Lakota coat the tips of their arrows in cow&nbsp;
manure, to ensure that their victims are killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:53.280
by infection if not from the wound itself. Despite&nbsp;
their medical attention and a spiritual ceremony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:57.840
it seems that there was truly&nbsp;
no saving the young Elsa Dutton.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:03.440
Having accepted his daughter's fate, James tells&nbsp;
Spotted Eagle of his intention to take Elsa into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.800
the mountains to die. He explains that wherever&nbsp;
Elsa lays is where he and his wife plan to lay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:14.000
claim and build a ranch. He goes on to say he does&nbsp;
not know this region, and fears it will not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:19.280
hospitable to him. In response, Spotted Eagle&nbsp;
suggests a valley he hunted while growing up.

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:26.960
"The winters are cruel, but the summers&nbsp;
are rich and a man who plans can thrive."

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:33.120
Spotted Eagle says it is called Paradise Valley,&nbsp;
which Yellowstone fans will recognize as the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:38.400
life valley where the series is set. It's also&nbsp;
the name of a capital investment firm, run by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:43.120
Dan Jenkins, that tries to push the Dutton's off&nbsp;
the Yellowstone more than a hundred years later.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.800
Spotted Eagle also gives James a word of&nbsp;
warning, telling him that in seven generations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:54.080
his people will rise up to reclaim their land.&nbsp;
The one thing he asks of James is permission to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:58.880
continue hunting in the valley, which he&nbsp;
happily gives. This also explains James'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:02.880
kindness towards a small tribe we saw&nbsp;
him meet in flashbacks in Yellowstone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.840
as he owes them a great debt for helping Elsa and&nbsp;
pointing them to their new home in the valley.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:12.080
Finally realizing that she won't&nbsp;
get better and will soon die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:17.760
Elsa feels betrayed that her father lied to her.&nbsp;
While speaking to her father, she requests to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:23.200
choose the spot where she is to die. James grants&nbsp;
her request, as Margaret watches from a distance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:28.160
distraught that her daughter has to face the truth&nbsp;
of her own demise. However, it's once again wisdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:33.280
from Shea Brennan that helps James come to terms&nbsp;
with Elsa's impending doom. The veteran soldier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:38.800
consoles him with the fact that Elsa lived a full&nbsp;
life, fuller than most, despite her young age.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:40.400
"She has outlived us all."

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.640
Elsa had a happy childhood and a loving&nbsp;
family. She traveled the plains, rode horses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:51.600
joined a wagon train, saw the beauty of the&nbsp;
land, and bonded with many of its inhabitants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.480
She clashed with Comanche and battled bandits;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.040 --> 00:10:00.640
she fell in love more than once, and was loved&nbsp;
back. Elsa had indeed lived the American Dream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:04.480
and death was now the last destination&nbsp;
on her short journey through this world.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:10.320
In "This Is Not Your Heaven," fans of Yellowstone&nbsp;
finally get the significant connections they'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:15.040
been looking for all season long. We learn&nbsp;
what pulled James Dutton off the path to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:20.640
Oregon to founding the Yellowstone Ranch. After&nbsp;
years of hints and references to family legacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:25.360
we finally understand the spark that ignited&nbsp;
the Dutton's passion for their home in Montana.

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:30.320
In an early part of the episode, Elsa muses to&nbsp;
her father that the only thing she fears about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:35.440
death is being forgotten. James and Margaret vow&nbsp;
that if she's to die, they will make their home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:40.960
where they bury her so they can visit her grave&nbsp;
every day, ensuring her memory. When Elsa chooses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:46.160
a spot by a tree near where the Dutton ranch&nbsp;
is to be built, audiences finally understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:51.520
the Dutton's ancestral claim to the land. We've&nbsp;
heard John Dutton's promise to his father not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:56.800
let the land go, and now we see that the same&nbsp;
promise has been handed down for generations.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:02.240
It seems the Dutton's claim to the Yellowstone&nbsp;
Ranch stretches back to 1883, with the death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:06.880
of Elsa Dutton. While her descendant&nbsp;
John Dutton refuses to relinquish it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:11.920
it remains to be seen if Spotted Eagle's prophecy&nbsp;
that his people will one day reclaim it is true.

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:16.000
Once Elsa passes away in her father's arms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:21.120
the story flashes forward one year. While we do&nbsp;
not see James and Margaret in their new ranch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:26.880
we do get updates on the surviving cast. Josef is&nbsp;
seen on crutches, his right leg amputated, as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:31.920
removes his wedding band in memory of his wife.&nbsp;
He pulls a stack of wood planks from his horse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:37.040
and we're left to believe that he is building his&nbsp;
new home in a valley. He is the lone survivor of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:41.600
his group, but he has finally found the home&nbsp;
he set out to build when the series began.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:47.120
We then cut to Willamette Valley, Oregon and see&nbsp;
Noemi and Thomas staking a claim on a plot of land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:52.320
near a river. Noemi's two children rush to&nbsp;
embrace her. This is especially meaningful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:56.320
because earlier we learned that her children&nbsp;
blamed her for the death of their father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:01.680
and hadn't spoken to her since. Finally in Oregon,&nbsp;
they build a new home and new life with Thomas.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:07.600
Finally, we see Shea on a beach, looking out&nbsp;
over the water. He speaks to his late wife Helen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:11.760
admiring the beauty of the Pacific&nbsp;
Ocean. Having completed his journey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:17.200
and seeing nothing left to hang on to, Shea ends&nbsp;
his own life while sitting on the shoreline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:24.240
Whether heartwarming or devastating, the ending&nbsp;
of 1883 won't leave a dry eye in the house.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:29.280
When the series began, audiences may have&nbsp;
suspected that 1883 would be a series very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:34.560
much like Yellowstone — a high octane drama&nbsp;
full of action and suspense. The Wild West&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:38.640
seemed like the perfect place for the&nbsp;
series' first spin-off, and surely fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:43.760
dreamed of rough-riding cowboys chasing down&nbsp;
bandits in a fast-paced western adventure.

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:48.960
When it premiered, viewers were instead treated&nbsp;
to a somber and emotional drama — a slower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:54.320
more intimate look at life on the frontier than&nbsp;
they may have expected. While James indeed played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:59.840
a central role, it became clear right away that&nbsp;
it would be Elsa Dutton's story, as she narrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:05.280
her journey north from Texas. An unexpectedly&nbsp;
subdued premiere episode gave way to a series that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:10.720
provided background to Yellowstone while telling&nbsp;
its own story that required no prior knowledge.

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:15.600
Perhaps the most surprising thing about&nbsp;
1883 is that it wasn't the story of hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:20.320
and discovery that many may have thought.&nbsp;
Instead, the series was ultimately a bleak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:25.440
tale of the hopelessness and sorrow felt by so&nbsp;
many as they ventured out onto the frontier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:29.200
Though there are moments of joy to&nbsp;
be found, even those are bittersweet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:34.160
as so many of the series' characters meet&nbsp;
untimely ends. The unforgiving narrative we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:39.920
left with illustrates how every element of early&nbsp;
American life was fraught with danger and death.

